Actor-billed LLM usage and credits

Status: not implemented. `generationCreditsAllowed` in
server/internal/httpapi/generation_credits.go is a stub that always allows.

Why this exists

Generation charges two users at once:

- The produced material is storage. It is billed to the workspace owner
  (`materials.owner_user_id`) and enforced by `gateStorageTx`. Done.
- The inference that produced it is model spend. It should be billed to the
  actor who requested it. Nothing tracks this.

Before this split was written down, `generate` called
`accountCreateAllowed(actor)`, which used the actor's storage lifecycle state as
a proxy for "may this user spend". That conflated two unrelated budgets and made
an over-quota editor unable to generate into a workspace whose owner had plenty
of room. See todo-permissions, Inconsistency 1. The check is now the stub
instead, so the intent is recorded at the call site rather than lost.

What a real implementation needs

- A spend ledger keyed by actor, not by workspace or owner. Generation is not
  the only future spender: chat, RAG retrieval, and re-generation all draw on
  the same budget, so the gate must take a cost estimate rather than being
  generation-specific.
- Tier allowances plus overage policy, derived from `user_subscriptions` the way
  `StorageLimitBytes` derives from `plan_tier`. Two budgets, one subscription.
- Reservation-then-settle, matching the upload reservation lifecycle in
  `server/internal/store/storage.go`: reserve an estimate before calling the
  model, settle the actual token cost after. A post-hoc-only ledger lets
  concurrent requests both pass the gate.
- A stable error code alongside `storage_quota_exceeded` so the frontend can
  tell "you are out of credits" from "the workspace owner is out of space".
  These render as completely different messages: one is about you, the other is
  about someone else.
- Pipeline enforcement. `generateViaPipe` reaches the retrieval service, which
  can spend without returning anything persistable, so a gate that only runs in
  Go is incomplete once the pipeline gains its own model calls.

Related deferred item: live collaboration connections are not downgraded when
their owner crosses into over-quota

Not credits, but recorded here because it is the other known deferral in the
same area.

Room access is decided once in `onAuthenticate`
(collaboration/src/server.ts) and Hocuspocus never re-authenticates, so
`connection.context.access` is fixed for the life of the socket. Collaboration
tokens live five minutes, which bounds how long a *new* connection can carry a
stale grant, but a socket opened while the owner was active keeps full write
access for as long as it stays connected.

Freeze is derived state, not a stored transition: `SweepOverQuotaNotices` in
server/internal/store/lifecycle_notices.go only sends mail, and nothing writes a
column when an account crosses `current_period_end`. So there is no event to
hang an eviction off yet.

The fix, when it is worth doing: have the sweeper publish
`evo:collaboration:evict` for the owner's materials the first time it observes
the account as shrink-only. `publishMaterialEviction` already exists in
server/internal/httpapi/huma_collaboration.go and is wired for privacy and
membership changes; the sweeper runs in the store package and would need Redis
access, which is the actual cost of the change.
